1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
That would mean the reflector joining a letter to itself, and it never does.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
So no letter is ever enciphered as itself. Press A, and you get any letter but A.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
We tried every key at every rotor position. Not one lit its own lamp.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
The plugboard can't change that: it swaps a letter on the way in, and swaps it back on the way out.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
How many ways could the army's Enigma be set up? Three rotors from a box of five, and ten cables.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Choosing the rotors and their order: five times four times three, or sixty ways.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Their starting positions: twenty-six times twenty-six times twenty-six, or seventeen thousand, five hundred and seventy-six.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Ten cables pairing up letters on the plugboard: about a hundred and fifty-one trillion ways.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Multiply them, and you get nearly a hundred and fifty-nine quintillion settings, even before the rotors' ring settings.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
At a million settings a second, trying them all would take about five million years.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
But nobody broke Enigma by trying every setting. They found shortcuts, and the first came from Poland.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
In nineteen twenty-nine, Poland's Cipher Bureau trained German-speaking mathematics students in Poznan, in secret.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
In September nineteen thirty-two, three of them joined the Bureau: Marian Rayefski, Yerzy Roozhitski, and Henrik Zigalski.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Rayefski spotted a German habit: each message began with its own rotor setting, typed twice, from a start shared that day.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
So the first and fourth letters hid the same letter, and so did the second and fifth, and the third and sixth.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
From a day's messages, those links join up into loops, which come in pairs of the same length.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
And the plugboard changes which letters sit in each loop, but never how long the loops are.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
A cryptology scholar later called this fact about permutations the theorem that won World War Two.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Rayefski still needed the rotors' wiring. Help came from a spy.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Hans-Thilo Schmidt, at Germany's Cipher Office, sold secrets to the French, including two months of keys from nineteen thirty-two.
